  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nSurface high and upper level ridging will push eastward tonight,\nbringing a southwest flow pattern aloft. This southwest flow\nwill advect in some lower level moisture increasing cloud cover\nfrom west to east. This should limit fog tonight and keep low\ntemperatures in the mid teens to mid 20s. A few flurries are\npossible from these low clouds although confidence is low.\nClipper system then moves lee of the northern Rockies on Tuesday\nand across the state. Lift from this low combined with the\nmentioned lower level moisture could provide for some snow\nshowers across the south central and southeast. Raised NBM pops\nto include these chances for snow showers in the mentioned\nareas. Snow accumulations should be light, with the chance for\naccumulation snow around 40% or less and a chance for an inch of\nsnow around 10% or less. The potential for banded snowfall looks\nfairly limited. There may be a decent jet streak, moisture\nstream, and lapse rates. However, the synoptic lift and\nfrontogensis look to be lacking. Otherwise look for slightly\nwarmer conditions on Tuesday, with highs in the 30s. Tuesday\nnight into Wednesday morning, low becomes cutoff and the low\nlevel moisture associated with the low stalls across the area.\nLight southerly flow will ensure this moisture stays in the\nregion as well. The result could be patchy to areas of fog. This\nis also indicated on hi-res guidance, thus have added it to the\nforecast. Lows Tuesday night will be somewhat dependent on fog\nand clouds, currently forecast in the mid teens to mid 20s.\n\nWednesday through Thanksgiving, zonal flow aloft will push the\ncutoff low eastward. This will bring warming temperatures and\nmainly dry conditions. Look for high temperatures in the 30s and\n40s each day, with lows in the 20s. Southerly flow will also\npersist during this time period. Perhaps some patchy fog is\npossible Thanksgiving morning, although the introduction of more\ndry air should help limit this potential. \n\nFriday into the weekend, transition to northwest flow aloft\ncould bring a broad trough and upper low pattern, with an\naccompany cold front. The result could be isolated to scattered\nrain and snow showers. Confidence in location and impacts with\nthese showers still remains uncertain and will be worth\nmonitoring going forward. Wind forecasts continue to increase\nfor Friday, with some breezy winds now possible into the\nweekend. Some near advisory level westerly winds are possible \nin the west Friday. Temperatures remain warm ahead of the wave\nand front on Friday, with highs in the 40s to perhaps lower 50s.\nWhere the front stalls over the weekend will determine\ntemperatures. Currently highs are forecast in the 30s and 40s,\nwhich would still be at least slightly above seasonable normals.\nLows generally in the 20s would also be above normal as well\nthrough the weekend. Abundant cloud cover may also be found at\ntimes given the northwest flow and upper low pattern.",
